To claim to have learned the language of a literate culture, you really
need to be able to understand the language in its "normal" written form
as well as the spoken, IMO.  I wouldn't claim to understand Russian, for
instance, unless I could read it when written in the Cyrillic alphabet as
well as being able to understand the spoken language. (After all, I
might need to read something, or write someone a note, or whatever.)

The "normal" written form for Japanese is for most words to be written
as their kanji, with certain words being written in hiragana (for
emphasis, IIRC) or katakana (usually because they're loanwords).

